Assuming you identify the /WoW immediately (otherwise the use of the wand
is delayed)...

If you intend to immediately use wishes in the early game to have a benefit
right away then I think you have to count differently.  The wand may have
1..3 charges (plus rechargability).  In early game you need to first wish
for scrolls of recharging to obtain your goal of immediate utilization.
Makes 0..2 charges left.  Then you have to identify the wand as to not risk
wresting out the last wish (been there); at that point you may or may not
have identify. If not then you should wait and will not have an immediate
gain from the wand (or play russian roulette and zap it).  If you have
identify it's easier and have 3..5(+1) charges for arbitrary items (not 7).

[BTW, the final (wrested) charge is also no option for "Immediate Problem
Solving", rather just for selection of some equipment.  And what equipment
would you chose?  You may also find it around the next corner.  Therefore
I usually not use it until I am in dare need of having something important
without that I would be unable to continue the game.]

Given these restrictions in early game *and* all that if/then/else *and*
the remaining variance *and* the low probability of the generation of a
/WoW then I question the importance of what we are talking about here.

That's for the "early" /WoW.

Finding one is a bright spot in a set of more or less lucky games, as the
wand of death in a gnomes hand is the opposite thereof.

